1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,186
Nadia: Welcome to Wired Planet, the show about the hidden machinery behind everyday technology.

2
00:00:05,406 --> 00:00:06,381
I'm Nadia.

3
00:00:06,931 --> 00:00:08,001
Omar: And I'm Omar.

4
00:00:08,221 --> 00:00:14,101
Today we're following a single web page from a server on the other side of the world all the way to your phone.

5
00:00:14,321 --> 00:00:18,781
Nadia, when most people picture the internet, what do they picture?

6
00:00:19,331 --> 00:00:21,231
Nadia: Usually something in the sky.

7
00:00:21,451 --> 00:00:23,685
Satellites, or a vague cloud.

8
00:00:23,905 --> 00:00:29,964
But the honest answer is that the internet is mostly glass and copper lying on the ground and on the sea floor.

9
00:00:30,184 --> 00:00:35,102
The cloud is really a lot of buildings full of computers, joined together by cables.

10
00:00:35,652 --> 00:00:38,320
Omar: So let's start at the bottom of the ocean.

11
00:00:38,540 --> 00:00:42,605
How much international traffic actually goes through undersea cables?

12
00:00:43,155 --> 00:00:44,581
Nadia: The vast majority.

13
00:00:44,801 --> 00:00:53,083
Industry estimates usually say well over ninety five percent of data travelling between continents goes through submarine fibre optic cables.

14
00:00:53,303 --> 00:01:02,935
Satellites carry a small share, and they matter a lot for remote places and ships, but cables carry the bulk because they offer far more capacity at lower cost.

15
00:01:03,485 --> 00:01:06,316
Omar: These cables are not a new idea, right?

16
00:01:06,866 --> 00:01:07,911
Nadia: Not at all.

17
00:01:08,131 --> 00:01:13,756
The first transatlantic telegraph cable was completed in 1858.

18
00:01:13,976 --> 00:01:19,722
Queen Victoria sent a message to the American president, and people celebrated in the streets.

19
00:01:19,942 --> 00:01:22,831
Then the cable failed after about three weeks.

20
00:01:23,051 --> 00:01:27,903
Engineers had pushed too much voltage through it, and the insulation broke down.

21
00:01:28,453 --> 00:01:29,521
Omar: Three weeks.

22
00:01:29,741 --> 00:01:31,161
That's a rough launch.

23
00:01:31,711 --> 00:01:34,313
Nadia: It is, but it taught a lasting lesson.

24
00:01:34,533 --> 00:01:42,346
A cable on the sea floor has to survive for decades with almost no maintenance, so it has to be designed carefully and treated gently.

25
00:01:42,566 --> 00:01:46,631
Modern cables are expected to last around twenty five years.

26
00:01:47,181 --> 00:01:49,480
Omar: What does a modern cable look like?

27
00:01:49,700 --> 00:01:52,444
I imagine something as thick as a tree trunk.

28
00:01:52,994 --> 00:01:57,295
Nadia: In the deep ocean, a typical cable is about as thick as a garden hose.

29
00:01:57,515 --> 00:02:00,963
At its heart are a few hair thin strands of glass fibre.

30
00:02:01,183 --> 00:02:08,069
Around them are layers of steel wire for strength, copper to carry electrical power, and plastic for insulation.

31
00:02:08,289 --> 00:02:15,896
Near the shore, where ships and fishing gear are a bigger risk, the cable gets extra armour and is often buried under the sea bed.

32
00:02:16,446 --> 00:02:20,324
Omar: Why does a cable need electrical power if it is carrying light?

33
00:02:20,874 --> 00:02:23,714
Nadia: Because light fades as it travels through glass.

34
00:02:23,934 --> 00:02:29,543
Every so often along the cable there is a repeater, which boosts the signal so it can keep going.

35
00:02:29,763 --> 00:02:34,905
Those repeaters need power, and that power is fed in from stations on land at each end.

36
00:02:35,455 --> 00:02:38,389
Omar: And the data itself is just pulses of light?

37
00:02:38,939 --> 00:02:39,678
Nadia: Yes.

38
00:02:39,898 --> 00:02:47,542
A laser switches on and off, or changes its pattern, billions of times per second, and each change encodes information.

39
00:02:47,762 --> 00:02:54,691
Engineers also send many colours of light down the same fibre at once, and each colour carries its own stream of data.

40
00:02:54,911 --> 00:03:02,020
That trick is called wavelength division multiplexing, and it is why a single fibre can carry an enormous amount of traffic.

41
00:03:02,570 --> 00:03:04,710
Omar: How fast does that light move?

42
00:03:05,260 --> 00:03:12,998
Nadia: Inside glass, light travels at roughly two thirds of its speed in a vacuum, so about two hundred thousand kilometres per second.

43
00:03:13,218 --> 00:03:17,064
That sounds instant, but over long distances it adds up.

44
00:03:17,284 --> 00:03:24,817
A signal crossing the Atlantic between London and New York needs a few tens of milliseconds each way, just because of distance.

45
00:03:25,037 --> 00:03:28,673
No amount of clever software can beat that physical limit.

46
00:03:29,223 --> 00:03:32,289
Omar: Okay, so we have this giant glass highway.

47
00:03:32,509 --> 00:03:35,433
But a web page isn't one big lump of light.

48
00:03:35,653 --> 00:03:37,699
How does it actually travel?

49
00:03:38,249 --> 00:03:41,584
Nadia: It gets chopped into small pieces called packets.

50
00:03:41,804 --> 00:03:47,630
Each packet carries a slice of the data plus a label saying where it came from and where it is going.

51
00:03:47,850 --> 00:03:54,826
Packets from the same page can take different routes and arrive out of order, and your device puts them back together.

52
00:03:55,376 --> 00:03:57,820
Omar: Who decided it should work that way?

53
00:03:58,370 --> 00:04:07,526
Nadia: The core rules come from a design described by Vint Cerf and Bob Kahn in 1974, which became the TCP and IP protocols.

54
00:04:07,746 --> 00:04:11,798
IP is the addressing system, a bit like the address on an envelope.

55
00:04:12,018 --> 00:04:15,052
TCP is the part that makes delivery reliable.

56
00:04:15,272 --> 00:04:22,115
Your device acknowledges the packets it receives, and if one goes missing, the sender notices and sends it again.

57
00:04:22,665 --> 00:04:26,850
Omar: So when a video call gets choppy, is that packets going missing?

58
00:04:27,400 --> 00:04:28,727
Nadia: Often, yes.

59
00:04:28,947 --> 00:04:38,040
For live calls, waiting for a lost packet to be resent would cause an awkward pause, so real time apps usually accept a little loss and keep going.

60
00:04:38,260 --> 00:04:44,995
That is why you might see a frozen frame or hear a robotic voice for a moment, rather than the call stopping completely.

61
00:04:45,545 --> 00:04:48,047
Omar: Let's go back to the start of the journey.

62
00:04:48,267 --> 00:04:50,835
I type a website name into my browser.

63
00:04:51,055 --> 00:04:52,600
What happens first?

64
00:04:53,149 --> 00:05:00,275
Nadia: Your device has to turn that name into a numeric address, because the network routes packets using numbers, not words.

65
00:05:00,495 --> 00:05:04,873
That job belongs to the Domain Name System, usually called DNS.

66
00:05:05,093 --> 00:05:14,809
It was introduced in 1983, and before that, computers relied on a single shared list of names and addresses that had to be copied around by hand.

67
00:05:15,359 --> 00:05:17,610
Omar: A single list for the whole internet?

68
00:05:17,830 --> 00:05:19,489
That can't have scaled.

69
00:05:20,039 --> 00:05:21,039
Nadia: It didn't.

70
00:05:21,259 --> 00:05:23,548
DNS replaced it with a hierarchy.

71
00:05:23,768 --> 00:05:33,721
There are root servers at the top, then servers responsible for endings like dot com or dot org, and then servers run by each organisation for its own names.

72
00:05:33,941 --> 00:05:44,970
Your device usually asks a nearby resolver, often run by your internet provider, and that resolver walks down the hierarchy and remembers the answer for a while so the next lookup is faster.

73
00:05:45,520 --> 00:05:48,912
Omar: So the phone book is spread out across the whole world.

74
00:05:49,462 --> 00:05:50,507
Nadia: Exactly.

75
00:05:50,727 --> 00:05:56,475
And because answers are remembered, or cached, most lookups never need to travel very far.

76
00:05:57,025 --> 00:06:03,160
Omar: Once my device knows the address, how do the packets find their way across all these different networks?

77
00:06:03,710 --> 00:06:06,371
Nadia: The internet is really a network of networks.

78
00:06:06,591 --> 00:06:12,675
Internet providers, universities, cloud companies and many others each run their own network.

79
00:06:12,895 --> 00:06:19,525
They tell each other which addresses they can reach using a protocol called the Border Gateway Protocol, or BGP.

80
00:06:19,745 --> 00:06:25,797
Each network then picks a path for traffic based on those announcements and on its business agreements.

81
00:06:26,347 --> 00:06:29,276
Omar: That sounds like it depends a lot on trust.

82
00:06:29,826 --> 00:06:32,868
Nadia: It does, and that is one of its weak points.

83
00:06:33,088 --> 00:06:38,411
In 2008, a provider in Pakistan tried to block YouTube inside the country.

84
00:06:38,631 --> 00:06:44,029
It announced a route for YouTube's addresses, and that announcement leaked out to the wider internet.

85
00:06:44,249 --> 00:06:51,594
For a couple of hours, a large share of the world's YouTube traffic was pulled toward the wrong network and simply disappeared.

86
00:06:52,144 --> 00:06:55,972
Omar: One mistake in one country, and the whole world notices.

87
00:06:56,522 --> 00:06:58,027
Nadia: That is the trade off.

88
00:06:58,247 --> 00:07:05,944
BGP is flexible and lets the network route around failures, but it was built in an era when operators mostly trusted each other.

89
00:07:06,164 --> 00:07:13,883
Today there are newer security measures that let networks check whether an announcement is legitimate, and adoption has been growing steadily.

90
00:07:14,433 --> 00:07:16,643
Omar: Here's something I've wondered about.

91
00:07:16,863 --> 00:07:23,517
If light needs time to cross the ocean, why does a popular website from another continent still load quickly?

92
00:07:24,067 --> 00:07:28,254
Nadia: Because very often the page isn't coming from the other continent at all.

93
00:07:28,474 --> 00:07:35,419
Large websites use content delivery networks, which keep copies of popular files on servers in many cities.

94
00:07:35,639 --> 00:07:40,191
When you ask for a video or an image, you are usually sent to a nearby copy.

95
00:07:40,411 --> 00:07:44,869
The original server might be far away, but the copy could be in your own city.

96
00:07:45,419 --> 00:07:49,931
Omar: So the fastest way to send data a long way is to not send it a long way.

97
00:07:50,481 --> 00:07:52,219
Nadia: That's a good way to put it.

98
00:07:52,439 --> 00:08:00,091
Caching close to the user saves time, and it also saves expensive long distance capacity for the traffic that really needs it.

99
00:08:00,641 --> 00:08:04,068
Omar: Then there's the last step, from the street to my phone.

100
00:08:04,618 --> 00:08:05,336
Nadia: Right.

101
00:08:05,556 --> 00:08:12,765
That last stretch might be fibre to your building, an older copper line, a cable television line, or a mobile network.

102
00:08:12,985 --> 00:08:18,818
If you are on wifi at home, the final few metres are radio waves between your router and your phone.

103
00:08:19,038 --> 00:08:24,862
Ironically, that short wireless hop is often the slowest and least reliable part of the whole journey.

104
00:08:25,412 --> 00:08:31,520
Omar: So when my video buffers, I should blame the wall between me and the router before I blame the ocean.

105
00:08:32,070 --> 00:08:33,845
Nadia: Quite often, yes.

106
00:08:34,065 --> 00:08:39,591
Moving closer to the router, or using a cable, fixes a surprising number of problems.

107
00:08:40,141 --> 00:08:42,578
Omar: Let's talk about things going wrong.

108
00:08:42,798 --> 00:08:45,378
What actually breaks undersea cables?

109
00:08:45,598 --> 00:08:47,571
I've heard sharks bite them.

110
00:08:48,121 --> 00:08:53,997
Nadia: Sharks have bitten cables, and there are photos to prove it, but they are a tiny part of the problem.

111
00:08:54,217 --> 00:09:02,387
Most faults are accidents caused by people, especially fishing gear dragged along the sea bed and ships dropping anchor in the wrong place.

112
00:09:02,607 --> 00:09:07,447
Natural events like earthquakes and underwater landslides cause some of the rest.

113
00:09:07,997 --> 00:09:12,173
Omar: And how do you fix a cable that's lying thousands of metres down?

114
00:09:12,723 --> 00:09:14,751
Nadia: With a specialised repair ship.

115
00:09:14,971 --> 00:09:20,441
The crew works out roughly where the fault is by sending test signals down the cable from shore.

116
00:09:20,661 --> 00:09:27,220
Then the ship lowers a hook called a grapnel, catches the cable, and lifts the damaged section to the surface.

117
00:09:27,440 --> 00:09:34,000
Technicians splice in a new piece on board, a very delicate job with glass fibres, and lower it back down.

118
00:09:34,220 --> 00:09:39,382
A repair can take days or weeks, depending on the weather and how deep the cable is.

119
00:09:39,932 --> 00:09:42,825
Omar: Does the internet go down while that happens?

120
00:09:43,375 --> 00:09:49,526
Nadia: Usually not, because important routes have several cables, and traffic is shifted onto the others.

121
00:09:49,746 --> 00:09:53,562
The problem is when a region depends on only one or two cables.

122
00:09:53,782 --> 00:10:01,996
Then a single fault can slow the connection for a whole country, which is why many governments now push for more routes and more landing points.

123
00:10:02,546 --> 00:10:06,206
Omar: Let's sum up for anyone who wants to remember the journey.

124
00:10:06,756 --> 00:10:07,517
Nadia: Sure.

125
00:10:07,737 --> 00:10:11,967
First, your device uses DNS to turn a name into a numeric address.

126
00:10:12,187 --> 00:10:17,315
Second, the page is split into packets, and TCP makes sure they all arrive.

127
00:10:17,535 --> 00:10:21,373
Third, networks use BGP to choose a path between them.

128
00:10:21,593 --> 00:10:28,698
Fourth, the long distances are covered by fibre optic cables, mostly on the sea floor, where light carries the data.

129
00:10:28,918 --> 00:10:35,667
And finally, a content delivery network often means the data didn't have to travel very far in the first place.

130
00:10:36,217 --> 00:10:37,874
Omar: And the practical tip?

131
00:10:38,424 --> 00:10:41,905
Nadia: If things feel slow, check the short hops first.

132
00:10:42,125 --> 00:10:47,506
Your wifi signal and your local connection are much more likely to be the bottleneck than the ocean.

133
00:10:48,056 --> 00:10:50,834
Omar: That's all for this episode of Wired Planet.

134
00:10:51,054 --> 00:10:57,466
Thanks for listening, and next time we'll look at how data centres keep millions of computers cool.
